## `allowJs` for mixed specs

During migration some files stay `.cy.js`. `"allowJs": true` in `cypress/tsconfig.json` lets the editor check JS with `// @ts-check`. Do not convert every spec in one PR unless you want a types-only firefight.

## Types for `cy.fixture`

```ts
type Employee = { email: string; role: 'employee' | 'manager' };

cy.fixture<Employee[]>('employees.json').then((employees) => {
  cy.wrap(employees[0].role).should('eq', 'employee');
});
```

Generic `fixture<T>` beats `any`. Keep fixture types next to the JSON or in `cypress/support/types.ts`.

## `esModuleInterop`

If `import { defineConfig } from 'cypress'` errors in config, set `"esModuleInterop": true` on whichever tsconfig covers the config file, or use `cypress.config.js`. Specs do not need to import `cypress` at all — `cy` is global via `types`.
## `strictNullChecks` and `.then`

```ts
cy.get('h1').invoke('text').then((text: string) => {
  expect(text.toLowerCase()).to.include('sign');
});
```

If `invoke('text')` is typed `string | number | ...`, narrow with `String(text)` or a `.should('match', /sign/i)` and skip the callback. Fighting the type is slower than using an implicit assertion.

## Global `cy` without import

You should **not** `import cy from 'cypress'`. Specs rely on globals from the `cypress` types package. Importing the `cypress` module in a spec is a config-file pattern (`defineConfig`), not a spec pattern.

## `jsx` in Cypress tsconfig

If a spec uses `.cy.tsx` (mounting is CT), set `"jsx": "react-jsx"`. Pure E2E `.cy.ts` files do not need jsx. Keep E2E as `.ts` unless you mount components there (you should not).
## `skipLibCheck`

Keep `"skipLibCheck": true` in `cypress/tsconfig.json`. Cypress's bundled types plus the app's `@types` can otherwise conflict inside `node_modules`. You still type-check **your** specs.

## IDE restart

After adding `cypress/tsconfig.json`, run "TypeScript: Restart TS Server". Stale roots are why `cy` stays unknown after you did everything right.
## `cypress` in `types` array must be alone with optional `node`

If you also list `"jest"`, `expect` becomes Jest's and `.should('be.visible')` still works (that is Cypress) but `expect(el).to.have.text` (Chai-jQuery) may break. Cypress + node only.

## The v10 replacement for plugins

Cypress 9:

```js
// cypress/plugins/index.js
module.exports = (on, config) => {
  on('task', { /* ... */ });
  return config;
};
```

Cypress 10+:

```ts
// cypress.config.ts
import { defineConfig } from 'cypress';

export default defineConfig({
  e2e: {
    setupNodeEvents(on, config) {
      on('task', {
        ping() {
          return 'pong';
        },
      });
      return config;
    },
  },
});
```

Delete unused `cypress/plugins/` after migrating so nobody adds new code there.

`setupNodeEvents` runs in **Node** when Cypress **opens the project** (each `cypress open` / `cypress run` process), not once per `it`. Closures can hold a DB pool. Do not assume a browser `window`.

## Signature and `return config`

```ts
setupNodeEvents(on, config) {
  // on — register listeners
  // config — resolved config (env already merged from files / CLI at this point)

  if (process.env.BASE_URL) {
    config.baseUrl = process.env.BASE_URL;
  }

  return config; // MUST return if you mutated; recommended even if you did not
}
```

If you mutate `config` and **do not return it**, Cypress keeps the pre-mutation object. Symptoms: `baseUrl` still localhost in CI, `env` flags ignored. **Always `return config`.**

`config.env` is the merged env **as of process start**. `CYPRESS_*` and `--env` are already applied. Runtime `Cypress.env()` in a spec happens later in the browser and does not loop back here.

## `cy.task` — the important plugin

Browser code cannot safely talk to Postgres or `fs`. A **task** is a named Node function Cypress invokes from the queue:

```ts
// setupNodeEvents
on('task', {
  seedEmployee({ email }: { email: string }) {
    // Node: insert into DB, or call an internal admin API with a server secret
    return { id: 'emp_123', email }; // PLAIN object
  },
  resetLeaveTable() {
    // ...
    return null; // Cypress forbids undefined — use null
  },
});
```

Spec:

```ts
cy.task('seedEmployee', { email: 'ada@bizlevate.test' }).then((emp) => {
  expect(emp.email).to.eq('ada@bizlevate.test');
});
cy.visit('/login');
```

`cy.task` **retries** until the function returns (subject to `taskTimeout`, default 60s). The handler may return a Promise:

```ts
async seedEmployee({ email }) {
  const row = await db.employees.create({ email });
  return { id: row.id, email: row.email }; // serialize!
}
```

### Serialization rules (memorize)

The return value crosses the **Node ↔ browser** boundary. It must be **JSON-serializable**:

| Return | OK? |
|---|---|
| `null` | Yes — use instead of `undefined` |
| `{ id: 1, name: 'Ada' }` | Yes |
| `['a', 'b']` | Yes |
| `'pong'` / `42` / `true` | Yes |
| `undefined` (implicit empty return) | **No** — Cypress errors |
| `() => {}` / class instance with methods | **No** |
| `Buffer`, `Map`, `Set`, `Date` (Dates often become strings if JSON'd — prefer ISO strings) | Avoid raw `Buffer`/`Map` |
| Mongoose document / Prisma object with circular refs | **No** — map to a plain object first |

```ts
// wrong
return employeeDocument;

// right
return {
  id: employeeDocument.id,
  email: employeeDocument.email,
};
```

**Interview line:** "`cy.task` must return serializable data. If there is no payload, return `null`. `setupNodeEvents` must return `config`."

## Tasks vs custom commands vs `cy.request`

| | Runs in | Use for |
|---|---|---|
| `Cypress.Commands.add` | Browser | UI sequences: `cy.login`, `cy.fillLeaveForm` |
| `cy.task` | Node | DB seed, filesystem, crypto, OS |
| `cy.request` | Node HTTP from Cypress backend | Hit HRM HTTP APIs with cookies/session |
| `cy.exec` | Node shell | Last resort scripts |

Prefer `cy.request` for "POST /api/test/seed" if HRM exposes a **test-only** HTTP route. Prefer `cy.task` when you must use a DB URL that should never be in the browser.

Never put database passwords in spec files. Task code can read `process.env.DATABASE_URL` in Node.

## Other `on(...)` events you will see

```ts
setupNodeEvents(on, config) {
  on('before:browser:launch', (browser, launchOptions) => {
    // launchOptions.args.push('--foo')
    return launchOptions;
  });

  on('after:spec', (spec, results) => {
    // delete passing videos, send metrics
  });

  on('task', { /* ... */ });

  return config;
}
```

Preprocessors (`file:preprocessor`) are how webpack/vite plugins used to work; modern Cypress has a default bundler. Do not add one until you need path aliases or Babel extras.

## Plugin *packages*

`setupNodeEvents` is also where you **call** npm plugins:

```ts
setupNodeEvents(on, config) {
  // example shape — grep the plugin's docs
  // require('cypress-grep/src/plugin')(config);
  return config;
}
```

Installing `cypress-image-snapshot` or similar still ends up registering here. You are not authoring a public plugin in this manual; you are **hooking Node events**.

## Playwright comparison

Playwright fixtures (`globalSetup`, `request`, custom fixtures) run in Node beside the test runner. Cypress splits **browser commands** vs **Node tasks** more sharply because the test body lives in the browser. That split is why `cy.task` exists at all.

Selenium: DB setup lives in `@Before` in Java — same process as the test language, no IPC serialization. Cypress tasks are IPC; serialization is the tax.

## Minimal HRM seed example

```ts
// cypress.config.ts (sketch)
setupNodeEvents(on, config) {
  on('task', {
    log(message: string) {
      console.log(message);
      return null;
    },
  });
  return config;
}
```

```ts
cy.task('log', 'seeding skipped in this chapter');
```

When you add a real DB task, unit-test the mapper that turns DB rows into `{ id, email }` so the serializable boundary stays boring.

## Failure modes

1. **`task` returned `undefined`** — add `return null`.
2. **Config change ignored** — you forgot `return config`.
3. **`cy.task('seedEmployee')` fails with "still pending"** — handler threw, or hung without resolving a Promise. Check terminal **Node** logs, not only the Command Log.
4. **Works in open, not in run** — task used a relative path that depends on cwd. Use `path.join(__dirname, ...)`.

